1 INITIALIZE

   Does one of two things, chosen by its qualifier, which must be given:

     INITIALIZE/CONTAINER   Makes a new, empty ODS-2 volume in a host
                            file, for MOUNT.
     INITIALIZE/VAX         Sets the size of the machine's physical
                            memory.

   INITIALIZE with neither qualifier is an error.  INIT is short for
   INITIALIZE.

2 /CONTAINER

   Makes a new, empty ODS-2 volume in a host file: a real Files-11
   volume, which VMS and other emulators such as simh can use too.  The
   volume isn't mounted; use MOUNT.

   Format

     INITIALIZE/CONTAINER container-file [volume-label]

3 Parameters

 container-file

   The new container's host file name.  Quote it if the host's file
   names are case sensitive, or if it has blanks.

 volume-label

   The label recorded in the volume's home block.  The default is
   NONAME.

3 Qualifiers
/CLUSTER=n

   The blocks in each storage cluster.  The default is 1.

/DEVICE=type

   Sizes the volume as a DEC disk of this type, such as RX50, RD51,
   RD54, RA81, or RA92.  Either /DEVICE or /SIZE must be given.

/SIZE=n

   The volume's size in 512-byte blocks.

3 Examples

   1.$ INITIALIZE/CONTAINER "project.dsk" /DEVICE=RD54 PROJECT

     Makes project.dsk, a 152 MB volume labeled PROJECT.

   2.$ INITIALIZE/CONTAINER "small.dsk" SMALL /SIZE=2000/CLUSTER=3

     Makes a volume of 2,000 blocks, in clusters of 3.

2 /VAX

   Sets the size of the machine's physical memory and clears the
   machine.  The memory is the govax process's own.

   Format

     INITIALIZE/VAX pages

3 Parameter

 pages

   The number of 512-byte pages of memory.  The number is in the
   console's radix, hexadecimal unless SET RADIX changed it: write
   ^D16384 for decimal.

3 Examples

   1.$ INITIALIZE/VAX ^D16384

     Gives the machine 8 MB of memory.
